  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/30-amphibians-and-reptiles/potamotyphlus_kaupii_en/",
      "html": "<p>Potamotyphlus kaupii is a fully aquatic caecilian (order Gymnophiona) that, unlike the uniformly black Typhlonectes natans, has an attractive colour pattern.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/30-amphibians-and-reptiles/potamotyphlus_kaupii_en/",
      "html": "<p>Only a few specimens could be imported so far. They proved to be much more sensitive than their black cousin from Colombia, and were especially sensitive to fungal infection of the skin. Once settled, however, they are as easy to keep as T. natans.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/30-amphibians-and-reptiles/potamotyphlus_kaupii_en/",
      "html": "<p>The specimens originate from Peru.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/30-amphibians-and-reptiles/potamotyphlus_kaupii_en/",
      "html": "<p>It is very important to cover the tank absolutely tight.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/30-amphibians-and-reptiles/potamotyphlus_kaupii_en/",
      "html": "<p>The eyeless animals orientate by scent and so find their prey. In the aquarium they are fed mainly frozen food such as bloodworms.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/30-amphibians-and-reptiles/potamotyphlus_kaupii_en/",
      "html": "<p>Caecilians have separate sexes; males have a kind of penis used for internal fertilisation. The water-living, livebearing species develop a kind of umbilical cord by which the unborn young are fed by the mother.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/30-amphibians-and-reptiles/potamotyphlus_kaupii_en/",
      "html": "<p>Caecilians are the third order of amphibians, after the Anura and Caudata, with more than 180 species. Most are nocturnal and live burrowed in the ground. Some genera live exclusively in freshwater; Typhlonectes natans from Colombia is sold as the \"blind eel\". Potamotyphlus: from ancient Greek \"potamon\" (river) and \"typhlos\" (blind), i.e. \"blind river dweller\". kaupii: dedication name.</p>"
    }
  ],
